summary:
The page first confirms the batch inspection from p.423: after preprocessing, **each sample is a vector of 20,000 numbers**, each tracking the presence or absence of one vocabulary term.

Next comes **a very simple linear model**, written as a function so it can be reused later.

**Listing 14.13 (Building a bag-of-words regression model)** defines `build_linear_classifier(max_tokens, name)`:

- `keras.Input(shape=(max_tokens,))`, a 20,000-dimensional input;
- one `layers.Dense(1, activation="sigmoid")`, which outputs the probability that the review is positive;
- `keras.Model(inputs, outputs, name=name)`;
- compiled with `optimizer="adam"`, `loss="binary_crossentropy"` and `metrics=["accuracy"]`.

The model is instantiated as `"bag_of_words_classifier"`. Its **summary** shows an `InputLayer` with output `(None, 20000)` and a `Dense` layer with output `(None, 1)` and **20,001 parameters**. Total params: 20,001 (78.13 KB), all trainable.

The authors call it 'dead simple': **one parameter per vocabulary word plus one bias**. In effect it is logistic regression, which is why the listing titles say 'regression model' even though the task is classification.

Training uses the **`EarlyStopping` callback from chapter 7**. It automatically stops training when the validation loss stops improving and restores the weights from the best epoch. **Listing 14.14** creates `EarlyStopping(monitor="val_loss", restore_best_weights=True, patience=2)` and begins `history = model.fit(`. The arguments of the call (the training Dataset, `validation_data`, `epochs=10`, `callbacks=[early_stopping]`) continue on p.425.
